# This file tells GitHub and citation tools (Zotero, Zenodo, etc.) how to
# cite HV_Harness. GitHub renders a "Cite this repository" button from it.
#
# It describes TWO things:
#   1. The software itself (the top-level fields below) — the tool a user ran.
#   2. The manuscript that describes it (the `preferred-citation` block) —
#      what you'd actually like people to cite in a paper.
#
# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ ONE VALUE STILL TO FILL IN, marked FILL-ME below:                   │
# │                                                                     │
# │   The preferred-citation DOI, once the preprint is posted.          │
# │   bioRxiv issues a DOI at the moment of posting, so this can be     │
# │   completed the same day. Change `journal` and add volume/pages     │
# │   if and when the paper is accepted at a journal.                   │
# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
#
# The two Zenodo DOIs under `identifiers` are not interchangeable:
#   - the CONCEPT DOI always resolves to the newest archived version;
#   - the VERSION DOI is pinned to this release and never moves.
# Cite the concept DOI for "the software"; cite the version DOI to pin
# the exact snapshot behind a result. When you tag a new release, add a
# new version-DOI entry and leave the concept DOI untouched.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use HV_Harness, please cite both the software and the paper below."
title: "HV_Harness: a human-AI workflow for synteny-grounded gene-family curation in allotetraploid carp genomes"
abstract: >-
  A structured human-AI collaboration protocol (a curation playbook plus
  supporting scripts) for building a synteny-grounded inventory and interactive
  hierarchy explorer of any gene group across the cyprinid Cs4R allotetraploid
  genomes (common carp, Prussian carp, goldfish), using zebrafish as a diploid
  comparator and publicly available NCBI-style annotation files only.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: John G.
    family-names: Walsh
    email: john.walsh@concordia.ab.ca
    affiliation: >-
      Department of Biological and Public Health Sciences, Concordia
      University of Edmonton, Edmonton, Alberta, Canada
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-7165-9398"
version: "1.0.1"
date-released: "2026-07-28"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/johnwalsh-collab/HV_Harness"
url: "https://github.com/johnwalsh-collab/HV_Harness"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21654547"
    description: >-
      Zenodo concept DOI — resolves to the latest archived version of
      HV_Harness. Use this to cite the software in general.
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21654548"
    description: >-
      Zenodo version DOI — pinned to HV_Harness v1.0.1, the release
      archived alongside the manuscript.
keywords:
  - allopolyploidy
  - allotetraploid
  - synteny
  - gene family
  - genome annotation
  - homeolog
  - Cyprinidae
  - common carp
  - goldfish
  - human-AI collaboration
  - bioinformatics

# The paper describing the workflow. Citation tools surface THIS as the
# preferred way to cite the method.
preferred-citation:
  type: article
  # Must match the posted title character-for-character (note the en dash
  # in "human–AI") so reference managers match it to the preprint record.
  title: >-
    Synteny over similarity: human–AI curation of the caspase family
    in allopolyploid carp genomes
  authors:
    - given-names: John G.
      family-names: Walsh
      orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-7165-9398"
  year: 2026
  journal: "bioRxiv"
  # doi: "10.1101/FILL-ME"      # <-- bioRxiv DOI, available on posting
